{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'squamous' src/ontology/cl-edit.owl\"","aggregated_output":"4525:# Class: obo:CL_0000076 (squamous epithelial cell)\n4528:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000076 \"squamous epithelial cell\")\n4533:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0721662544\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:26106328\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000077 \"A flat, squamous-like epithelial cell of mesodermal origin. It forms the mesothelium, which lines the body's serous cavities including the pleural, peritoneal, and pericardial spaces. This cell plays a crucial role in synthesizing and secreting lubricants, such as glycosaminoglycans and surfactants, which minimize friction between adjacent tissues during movement.\")\n4536:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:26106328\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000077 \"Mesothelial cells, which morphologically resemble squamous epithelial cells, express both epithelial markers (cytokeratins) and mesenchymal markers (vimentin), reflecting their mesodermal origin. They feature surface microvilli and apical-basal polarity. Under certain conditions, they can undergo mesothelial-to-mesenchymal transition. These cells also produce extracellular matrix proteins, maintain serosal homeostasis, and facilitate fluid transport.\")\n4906:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MESH:D042783\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:21275341\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Endothelial_cell\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://sourceforge.net/tracker/?func=detail&atid=440764&aid=3364936&group_id=36855\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000115 \"An endothelial cell comprises the outermost layer or lining of anatomical structures and can be squamous or cuboidal. In mammals, endothelial cell has vimentin filaments and is derived from the mesoderm.\")\n4911:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000115 \"From FMA: 9.07.2001: Endothelial cell has always been classified as a kind of epithelial cell, specifically a squamous cell but that is not true. First, endothelial cell can either be squamous or cuboidal (e.g. high-endothelial cell) and secondly, it has different embryological derivation (mesodermal) than a true epithelial cell (ectodermal and endodermal). The basis for present classification is the fact that it comprises the outermost layer or lining of anatomical structures (location-based) but a better structural basis for the differentia is the cytoskeleton of the cell. Endothelial cell has vimentin filaments while an epithelial cell has keratin filaments. [Onard].\")\n6093:# Class: obo:CL_0000240 (stratified squamous epithelial cell)\n6095:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30422572\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"Wikipedia:Epithelium\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"Wikipedia:Stratified_squamous_epithelium\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000240 \"A stratified epithelial cell that is part of squamous epithelium, characterized by multiple layers of cells. The basal layer is directly attached to the basement membrane and the apical layer consists of flattened squamous cells. This provides a protective barrier, commonly found in areas subject to abrasion, such as the skin, oral cavity, and esophagus.\")\n6096:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000240 \"stratified squamous epithelial cell\")\n6129:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0721662544\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000244 \"A cell characteristically found lining hollow organs that are subject to great mechanical change due to contraction and distention; originally thought to represent a transition between stratified squamous and columnar epithelium.\")\n6589:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:15749908\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19256306\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19727116\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:29713660\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:37873034\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000312 \"An epithelial cell of stratified squamous tissues, including skin, oral mucosa (Hiroshima et al., 2011), and esophagus (Whelan et al., 2018), that produces keratin proteins and secretes antimicrobial peptides to form a resilient barrier against environmental damage, dehydration, pathogens, and microbial invasion. It undergoes successive stages of differentiation marked by changes in keratin expression, supporting tissue integrity, wound repair, and contributing to immune defense.\")\n14627:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:20054144\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:29463737\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://www.copewithcytokines.de\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002062 \"A squamous pulmonary alveolar epithelial cell that is flattened and branched. A pulmonary alveolar type 1 cell covers more than 98% of the alveolar surface. This large cell has thin (50-100 nm) cytoplasmic extensions to form the air-blood barrier essential for normal gas exchange.\")\n14636:AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0002062 \"squamous alveolar cell\")\n14637:AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0002062 \"squamous alveolar lining cell\")\n14651:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0412046911\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:36138472\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:8540632\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://www.copewithcytokines.de\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002063 \"A pulmonary alveolar epithelial cell that modulates the fluid surrounding the alveolar epithelium by secreting and recycling surfactants via specialized organelles called alveolar lamellar bodies. This cell type also contributes to tissue repair and can differentiate after injury into a pulmonary alveolar type 1 cell. This cuboidal cell is thicker than squamous alveolar cells, has a rounded apical surface that projects above the level of the surrounding epithelium, and its free surface is covered by short microvilli.\")\n15664:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:068340007X\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002153 \"The dead keratin-filled squamous cell of the stratum corneum. This cell type lacks a nucleus.\")\n16046:# Class: obo:CL_0002190 (squamous cell of epidermis)\n16052:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0002190 \"squamous cell of epidermis\")\n16387:# Class: obo:CL_0002221 (keratinized squamous cell of esophagus)\n16389:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:11694559\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002221 \"A squamous cell that has keratin in the esophagus.\")\n16393:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0002221 \"keratinized squamous cell of esophagus\")\n16633:# Class: obo:CL_0002244 (squamous cell of ectocervix)\n16635:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32644501\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"doi:/10.1016/j.autneu.2015.04.008\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://www.bu.edu/histology/p/19404loa.htm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002244 \"A stratified squamous epithelial cell located in the ectocervix.\")\n16639:AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0002244 \"ectocervical squamous cell\")\n16640:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0002244 \"squamous cell of ectocervix\")\n16788:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0517223651\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31869123\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002258 \"An epithelial cell that is part of the thyroid follicle epithelium, with a shape that ranges from squamous when inactive to cuboidal or columnar when active, reflecting its hormonal activity. Its apical surface is lined with microvilli projecting into the colloid-filled lumen, where it synthesizes, stores, and secretes the thyroid hormones thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3), releasing them into the bloodstream in response to thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH).\")\n17479:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:14592528\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002318 \"A mesothelial cell capable of circulating in the blood by first losing its squamous character. This cell can incorporate into the regenerating mesothelium.\")\n17689:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:15339667\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:20568222\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9520465\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002337 \"A stem cell found in the basal layer of the epidermis, hair follicle, and sebaceous gland, characterised by its quiescent nature, high proliferative potential, and capacity for long-term self-renewal. This cell maintains epidermal homeostasis by producing transit-amplifying cells that differentiate to replenish the stratified squamous epithelium.\")\n20765:# Class: obo:CL_0002653 (squamous endothelial cell)\n20767:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:dos\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002653 \"A squamous shaped endothelial cell.\")\n20770:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0002653 \"squamous endothelial cell\")\n23131:# Class: obo:CL_0008040 (squamous endothelial cell of venule)\n23133:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0008040 \"An endothelial cell of the venule that is squamous shaped. This is in contrast to the cubodial shape of high endothelial venule cells.\")\n23136:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0008040 \"squamous endothelial cell of venule\"@en)\n23956:# Class: obo:CL_0009066 (stratified squamous epithelial cell of anal canal)\n23958:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://orcid.org/0000-0003-3440-1876\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0009066 \"A stratified squamous epithelial cell that is part of the anal canal.\")\n23960:AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0009066 \"anal canal stratified squamous epithelial cell\")\n23963:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0009066 \"stratified squamous epithelial cell of anal canal\")\n24265:# Class: obo:CL_0009096 (esophagus non-keratinized squamous epithelial cell)\n24267:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:11694559\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://orcid.org/0000-0003-2034-601X\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0009096 \"Any cell that is part of the esophageal non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium. In humans, the esophagus, which requires flexibility to accommodate swallowing of a bolus, is covered with a non-keratinized epithelium.\")\n24271:AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0009096 \"esophageal non-keratinized squamous epithelial cell\")\n24272:AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0009096 \"esophageal nonkeratinized squamous epithelial cell\")\n24273:AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0009096 \"esophagus non-keratinizing squamous epithelial cell\")\n24274:AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0009096 \"esophagus nonkeratinizing squamous epithelial cell\")\n24275:AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0009096 \"non-keratinized squamous epithelial cell of esophagus\")\n24276:AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0009096 \"non-keratinizing squamous epithelial cell of esophagus\")\n24277:AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0009096 \"nonkeratinized squamous epithelial cell of esophagus\")\n24278:AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0009096 \"nonkeratinizing squamous epithelial cell of esophagus\")\n24279:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0009096 \"esophagus non-keratinized squamous epithelial cell\")\n26120:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"FMA:256516\") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_1000298 \"squamous mesothelial cell of dura mater\")\n28770:# Class: obo:CL_1001575 (uterine cervix squamous cell)\n28774:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"CALOHA:TS-1251\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_1001575 \"cervix squamous cell\")\n28775:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"CALOHA:TS-1251\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_1001575 \"cervix squamous epithelial cell\")\n28776:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"CALOHA:TS-1251\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_1001575 \"cervix, uterine squamous epithelial cells\")\n28777:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"CALOHA:TS-1251\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_1001575 \"uterine cervix squamous epithelial cell\")\n28778:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"CALOHA:TS-1251\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_1001575 \"uterine cervix squamous epithelial cells\")\n28779:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_1001575 \"uterine cervix squamous cell\")\n28783:# Class: obo:CL_1001576 (oral mucosa squamous cell)\n28787:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"CALOHA:TS-1252\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_1001576 \"oral cavity mucosa squamous cell\")\n28788:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"CALOHA:TS-1252\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_1001576 \"oral cavity mucosa squamous epithelial cell\")\n28789:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"CALOHA:TS-1252\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_1001576 \"oral mucosa squamous epithelial cell\")\n28790:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"CALOHA:TS-1252\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_1001576 \"oral mucosa squamous epithelial cells\")\n28791:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_1001576 \"oral mucosa squamous cell\")\n28794:# Class: obo:CL_1001577 (tonsil squamous cell)\n28798:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"CALOHA:TS-1253\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_1001577 \"tonsil squamous epithelial cell\")\n28799:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"CALOHA:TS-1253\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_1001577 \"tonsil squamous epithelial cells\")\n28800:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"CALOHA:TS-1253\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_1001577 \"tonsillar squamous cell\")\n28801:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"CALOHA:TS-1253\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_1001577 \"tonsillar squamous epithelial cell\")\n28802:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_1001577 \"tonsil squamous cell\")\n28806:# Class: obo:CL_1001578 (vagina squamous cell)\n28810:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"CALOHA:TS-1254\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_1001578 \"vagina squamous epithelial cell\")\n28811:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"CALOHA:TS-1254\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_1001578 \"vagina squamous epithelial cells\")\n28812:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"CALOHA:TS-1254\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_1001578 \"vaginal squamous cell\")\n28813:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"CALOHA:TS-1254\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_1001578 \"vaginal squamous epithelial cell\")\n28814:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_1001578 \"vagina squamous cell\")\n31888:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref <https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7772-9578>) Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30069044\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32726565\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33442032\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4030023 \"A hillock cell that is located in respiratory epithelium. In some mammalian species, this cell type has been noted to express KRT13 and KRT4 and is postulated to play a role in squamous barrier function and immunomodulation.\")\n31897:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30069044\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4030024 \"An epithelial, transitional cell type between basal and secretory; located in stratified, non-ciliated structures (called hillocks) with high cell turnover in epithelium. In some mammalian species, this cell type has been noted to express KRT13 and is postulated to play a role in squamous barrier function and immunomodulation.\")\n32470:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:22375063\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35883599\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9520465\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://www.proteinatlas.org/humanproteome/single+cell+type/squamous+epithelial+cells#suprabasalkeratinocytes\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033013 \"A keratinocyte that resides in the epidermal suprabasal layer and expresses differentiation markers, including keratin 1 and keratin 10, in both humans and mice. In human interfollicular epidermis, this cell retains proliferative capacity and possesses retrodifferentiation potential, acquiring basal-like properties during wound healing or basement membrane contact.\")\n33090:# Class: obo:CL_4033083 (squamous granulosa cell)\n33092:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:28892263\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033083 \"A granulosa cell that has a squamous morphology and form a single layer around the oocyte in primordial follicles. This cell develops directly into a cuboidal granulosa cell during the primordial-to-primary follicle transition.\")\n33097:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33914868\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType obo:OMO_0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_4033083 \"squamous GC\")\n33098:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6677-8489\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_4033083 \"Often in literature squamous granulosa cell and pre-granulosa cell are used as synonyms. However, this term only makes reference to the quiescent granulosa cells that surround the primordial ovarian follicle, while a pre-granulosa cell proliferates to form the primordial follicle (and thus cells become the squamous granulosa cells).\")\n33099:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4033083 \"squamous granulosa cell\")\n33107:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:28892263\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033084 \"A granulosa cell that has a cuboidal morphology and develops from squamous granulosa cell during the transition between primordial follicle to primary follicle. Cuboidal granulosa cells proliferate to form a second layer within secondary follicles.\")\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
